/*!
    \page security.html
    \title Security in Qt
    \brief Addressing security issues in Qt
    \ingroup security

    Qt's security relies on the infrastructure created and maintained by
    the Qt Group and Qt Project. This infrastructure involves the development,
    testing, and build environments. For example, there is an established code
    review process, a testing process using static analyzers and fuzzing tools,
    testing of third-party components, and further antivirus testing for each
    release. Qt also has an established process for handling security
    vulnerabilities.

    The \e{security of an application or device that uses Qt} is a shared
    responsibility between the application developers, the Qt Group and
    Qt Project as providers of the Qt Framework, and operators or device
    manufacturers. For an overview of the respective obligations and
    boundaries, see \l{Qt Shared Security Model}.

    \section1 Qt Project security policy

    The Qt Project specifies its security policy in
    \l{https://contribute.qt-project.org/quips/15}{QUIP 15}.
    A summary of the security policy:

    \list
        \li Qt has a \e{Core Security Team} that enforces the security policy
            and addresses issues.
        \li Proactive measures to prevent security issues - code reviews, code
            analysis, fuzz testing, and so on.
        \li Reporting Security Issues: the Core Security Team monitors security
            issues for Qt modules and affected third-party components.
        \li Handling Security Issues: the maintainers, Core Security Team,
            Chief Maintainer, and Qt Group share and handle security
            issues.
        \li Disclosure of confirmed security issues at
            \l{https://cve.mitre.org/}{Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures database}
            and a public announcement to the Qt
            \l{mailto:announce@qt-project.org} mailing list.

            You can subscribe to the mailing list by visiting the
            \l{https://lists.qt-project.org/listinfo/announce}
              {Qt Project Mailing List Information Page}.
    \endlist

    \section1 Reporting security issues

    To report security issues in \l{https://www.qt.io/product}{Qt Products},
    send an email to Security Mail List at \l{mailto:security@qt-project.org}.
    The Core Security Team monitors and moderates incoming emails on business
    days (excluding weekends).
    After sending an email to the Security Mail List, there will be an
    acknowledgment of receipt within
    two business days. If there is no response, then the reporter should
    contact the
    \l{https://wiki.qt.io/Maintainers}{Chief Maintainer} directly.

    To report issues regarding Qt Group services such as the website or
    Qt Account, email \l{mailto:security@qt.io}.

    Qt does not currently have a bug bounty program.

    \section2 Qt Group commercial security agreement

    For commercial licensees, use the \e{Security Issues} category in the
    support portal to report issues to the Qt Group Support team. The
    reporter will be sent an acknowledgment when the issue is forwarded to the
    Security Mail List.

    Visit the
    \l{https://www.qt.io/terms-conditions/responsible-vulnerability-disclosure-process}{Responsible Vulnerability Disclosure Agreement} page for more information.

    \section1 Qt vulnerabilities

    When technical experts confirm a security issue in one of the Qt products,
    a Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures entry (CVE) will be
    created. A CVE consists of an ID and a description. Further metadata
    is added over time.

    For Qt products, The Qt Company is managing the CVE creation process as the
    responsible \l{https://www.cve.org/PartnerInformation/ListofPartners/partner/TQtC}
    {CVE Numbering Authority (CNA)}. It lists new CVEs in the
    \l{https://wiki.qt.io/List_of_known_vulnerabilities_in_Qt_products}
    {List of known vulnerabilities in Qt Products}, but also contributes to
    the \l{https://www.cve.org/}{central CVE database}.

    To uniquely identify products affected by a vulnerability, CVEs contain entries
    in the \e{Common Platform Enumeration} (CPE) format. For issues in the Qt Framework, the
    \e vendor and \e product parts of such a CPE are \c qt:qt.
    For example, a hypothetical CVE that affects Qt \QtVersion will have the following CPE:

    \badcode \QtVersion
    cpe:2.3:a:qt:qt:\1:*:*:*:*:*:*:*
    \endcode

    \section1 Software Bill of Materials (SBOM)

    Starting from Qt 6.8, the Qt installation includes Software Bill of
    Materials (SBOM) documents, containing information about installed Qt
    modules, packages, and third-party components in SPDX format. Since
    Qt 6.11, the SBOM documents are also available in CycloneDX format.
    Use the documents in either of these formats to track Qt installed
    packages for vulnerability management and license compliance.

    \list
        \li \l{Software Bill of Materials}
        \li \l{https://spdx.dev/learn/overview/#producing}{SPDX Format Overview}
        \li \l{https://cyclonedx.org/specification/overview/}{CycloneDX Specification Overview}
    \endlist

    \section1 Untrusted data

    Several Qt modules handle data such as user input and executable resources.
    Qt expects application developers to handle untrusted data appropriately.
    If a Qt API fetches and processes untrusted data before the application
    could do the processing, then Qt considers this API as
    \e{security critical}. Security critical APIs undergo extra scrutiny and
    testing during development.

    In general, avoid unprocessed data from unknown sources if possible and
    perform safety procedures when handling data. Qt provides several
    mechanisms for processing data, such as validators for user input.

    For more information, see \l{Handling Untrusted Data}.

    \section1 Developer tools

    The Qt Framework ships developer tools that run on your build machine
    while building a Qt application, such as \l{moc}{Meta-Object Compiler (moc)},
    \l{rcc}{Resource Compiler (rcc)}, and the various code and asset generators.
    These tools treat the project they process as trusted input and are not
    a security boundary. Building a project you do not trust can therefore
    lead to code execution on your build machine.

    For more information, see \l{Using Qt's Developer Tools Securely}.

    \section1 Permissions

    Qt 6.5 introduces a cross-platform permission API for handling permissions.
    The permission API is for user-related private data and hardware such as
    contact lists, calendar, camera, and microphone.

    For more information, see \l{Application Permissions}.

    \section1 Extended Security Maintenance

    We acknowledge that your product may have a lifecycle that is longer than
    our usual support window for a given Qt release. We now offer maintenance
    services for customers with an end-of-support (EoS) version of Qt.

    The Extended Security Maintenance subscription service provides you access
    to Critical Vulnerability and Security (CVS) maintenance patches relating to
    Qt libraries for select \l{lts-releases}{Long Term Support (LTS) releases}
    after the end-of-support (EoS).

    The ESM service is available for the following versions:

    \table
    \header
        \li Version
        \li ESM since
    \row
        \li Qt 5.15 LTS
        \li 2025-05-26
    \row
        \li Qt 6.5 LTS
        \li 2026-03-30
    \endtable

    For more information, see \l {https://www.qt.io/qt-support/} {Qt Support}.
*/
